 ### 12A.8 Rules for all admin RPCs
 
 - Every admin RPC: (a) runs `is_platform_admin()`; (b) requires a non-empty reason for grants, revocations, replacements, and reversals; (c) requires `confirm = true`; (d) requires `expected_state_version`; (e) requires a `request_id` (uuid), globally unique (§13, 2026-11-02 item 8). A repeated `request_id` with the same payload hash returns the original result without a second change. The same `request_id` with a different payload is a conflict (R5). (f) writes an audit row in the same transaction; (g) on denial, returns `{ok: false, code: 'forbidden'}` as a committed result, not a raised error, so the denial record and the denial counter (`licence_denial_windows`, O1) survive (R3). The same code is returned for every denial reason. Detailed denial records and their cap are in §12B.7 (M3). *Scope narrowed 2026-11-04 (A4):* this forbidden result covers authorisation failures only. See the A4 bullet below.
-- *Response contract (A4, decided 2026-11-04 by owner):* (i) An authorisation failure returns `{ok: false, code: 'forbidden'}` as a committed result, is logged to `licence_denials`, and counts toward the authorisation-denial counter (`licence_denial_windows`). Authorisation failures include a failed `is_platform_admin()` check, a failed `aal2` check (read as 'denied' in §12A.11), and a shop owner or staff caller of a write RPC. (ii) Every other rejection, whether validation or business (missing reason, unconfirmed call, stale `expected_state_version`, backdating without confirmation, revocation after the natural end (M2), a request ID reused with a different payload (R5), or an invalid custom range), returns a specific committed code. It is not counted as an authorisation denial and writes no denial row. Specific code names are set in implementation review. Whether these rejections write a replay-ledger row is OPEN (§13, 2026-11-04 Stage 7C record).
